WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:10.267
Welcome! This tutorial shows how to use the code of our paper, Robust parametric estimation of avian cranial morphology.

2
00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:17.194
The code measures 3D skull meshes automatically, without placing any landmarks by hand.

3
00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:28.062
It measures the size of the skull with a bounding box, finds the orbit from the curvature of the surface and fits a sphere to it, and fits an ellipsoid to the braincase.

4
00:00:28.112 --> 00:00:34.318
The same code also works on other skulls, such as Hawaiian honeycreepers and human crania.

5
00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:43.811
In part one, you will measure your own skulls: download the code, install it, add your meshes, run the fitting, and check the results.

6
00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:49.159
In part two, you will reproduce the numbers, tables and figures of the paper.

7
00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:59.488
You need Python 3.12. A code editor such as PyCharm or VS Code makes things easier, but it is not required.

8
00:00:59.538 --> 00:01:05.573
You do not need to program: you will change a line or two at the top of a script, and press Run.

9
00:01:06.423 --> 00:01:17.901
First, download the code. On the GitHub page of the project, click the green Code button, then Download ZIP, and unzip the folder anywhere on your computer.

10
00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:21.724
If you use git, you can clone the repository instead.

11
00:01:22.574 --> 00:01:24.337
Here is what you get.

12
00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:29.634
The data folder holds all the skull meshes, and the data tables of the paper.

13
00:01:29.684 --> 00:01:39.641
The numbered folders hold the scripts, one folder for each step: remeshing, fitting, statistics, the two orbits, the figures, and the verification.

14
00:01:39.691 --> 00:01:47.581
Every script finds the meshes through the small file paths.py, so you can run any script from its own folder.

15
00:01:48.431 --> 00:01:59.163
Next, install the Python packages. Open a terminal in the project folder, and type: pip install -r requirements.txt

16
00:01:59.213 --> 00:02:08.351
This installs everything the scripts need, such as trimesh for the meshes and PyVista for the 3D views. You only do this once.

17
00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:11.631
Part one: measure your own skulls.

18
00:02:13.081 --> 00:02:15.490
Before you start, check your meshes.

19
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:22.511
Each skull must be a triangle mesh in STL format, in millimetres, with one skull per file.

20
00:02:22.561 --> 00:02:34.483
Orient the skulls as ours are: the x axis runs along the skull, with the beak towards minus x and the back of the skull towards plus x, and the z axis points up.

21
00:02:34.533 --> 00:02:43.931
The settings also assume remeshed surfaces, with about 0.8 millimetres between vertices, like the meshes in the data folder.

22
00:02:43.981 --> 00:02:54.223
Raw scans must be remeshed first: the scripts in the folder 1_remeshing do this, using MATLAB and the remeshing code described in its read-me.

23
00:02:55.073 --> 00:03:02.182
Now make a new folder inside data, for example My_skulls, and copy your STL files into it.

24
00:03:03.032 --> 00:03:06.957
Open the script fit_sphere_batch.py, in the folder 2_fitting.

25
00:03:07.007 --> 00:03:11.592
Near the top, the list DIRECTORY_PATHS gives the folders to measure.

26
00:03:11.642 --> 00:03:17.394
Replace these folders with yours, as shown. You can list as many folders as you like.

27
00:03:18.244 --> 00:03:24.515
Now press Run in your editor, or type python fit_sphere_batch.py in a terminal.

28
00:03:24.565 --> 00:03:33.721
For each skull, the script looks for the most concave surface in the middle of the skull, fits a sphere to it, and prints the radius in millimetres.

29
00:03:33.771 --> 00:03:37.124
Here, all six skulls were fitted in less than a minute.

30
00:03:37.974 --> 00:03:43.102
The results are written next to the script, in the folder output/fit_sphere_batch.

31
00:03:43.152 --> 00:03:51.107
You get one Excel table for all the skulls, a picture of every fit, and a list of the skulls where no orbit was found.

32
00:03:51.957 --> 00:03:54.712
In the table, each row is one skull.

33
00:03:54.762 --> 00:03:59.630
Length, width and height are the sides of the bounding box, in millimetres.

34
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.959
The sphere radius is the radius of the orbit, and the curvature is one over the radius.

35
00:04:06.009 --> 00:04:16.264
n_inliers is the number of surface points the sphere was fitted to, and rms_over_radius is the fit error, as a fraction of the radius.

36
00:04:17.114 --> 00:04:29.183
Always check the fits. In the paper, a fit is accepted when it uses at least 40 points, and its fit error is at most 0.10, that is, ten percent of the radius.

37
00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:37.822
Then open the pictures. In a good fit, the blue sphere sits in the orbit, and the red points it was fitted to line the orbit wall.

38
00:04:37.872 --> 00:04:46.250
Here, the fit error is 0.18, above the limit: the points spread over the rim of the orbit, and this fit is flagged.

39
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.926
The default settings are made for finch skulls.

40
00:04:49.976 --> 00:04:59.628
The orbit is searched for in the middle of the skull, from 30 to 70 percent of its length, and its radius must be between 2 and 6 millimetres.

41
00:04:59.678 --> 00:05:06.778
For other animals, change these values here, or give a folder its own values in FOLDER_OVERRIDES.

42
00:05:06.778 --> 00:05:13.262
For the human crania of the paper, we used 10 to 50 percent, and 2 to 60 millimetres.

43
00:05:14.112 --> 00:05:22.258
To fit the ellipsoid to the braincase, open fit_ellipsoid.py in the same folder, and set DIRECTORY_PATH to your folder.

44
00:05:22.308 --> 00:05:31.792
It uses the most convex surface in the back 40 percent of the skull, and writes the three semi-axes of every skull to an Excel table.

45
00:05:31.842 --> 00:05:41.626
A 3D window shows each fit: close it to go on to the next skull, or set ENABLE_VISUALIZATION to False to run without windows.

46
00:05:42.476 --> 00:05:44.900
Part two: reproduce the paper.

47
00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:54.003
The data folder already contains the 100 skulls of Darwin's finches and their relatives used in the paper.

48
00:05:54.053 --> 00:06:04.276
Run fit_sphere.py and fit_ellipsoid.py in the fitting folder, and you get the measurements of the file Dataset.xlsx, to within a millionth of a millimetre.

49
00:06:05.126 --> 00:06:08.235
The statistics folder reads these measurements.

50
00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:19.086
correlation.py relates the orbit to the size of the skull, and modelling.py fits Equation 8, which predicts the curvature of the orbit from the three dimensions of the skull.

51
00:06:19.936 --> 00:06:29.104
two_orbit.py fits the orbit on both sides of every skull, and draws both fits, as in Section S5 of the Supporting Information.

52
00:06:29.954 --> 00:06:37.056
figure.py draws every figure of the paper from the meshes, as vector PDF files.

53
00:06:37.106 --> 00:06:41.140
Before drawing, it checks 22 numbers against the paper.

54
00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:51.211
Finally, verify_all.py refits every mesh, recomputes every number and table of the paper, and compares each one with the printed value.

55
00:06:51.261 --> 00:06:55.074
It writes a report that you can open in any web browser.

56
00:06:55.924 --> 00:07:02.044
Every script writes its results into an output folder next to it, and never changes your data.

57
00:07:02.094 --> 00:07:06.392
If you use the code, please cite our paper. Thank you for watching!
